# 🔍 MITS Backend — Full Audit Report
**Date:** 2026-03-04 | **Stack:** Node.js · Express 5 · MongoDB · Mongoose · JavaScript (no TypeScript)

---

## Executive Summary

The backend is a clean, readable Express REST API organized around 7 role-based resource groups. The architecture is a reasonable MVC-lite pattern (controllers + models + routes) with a thin service layer. For an early-stage academic portal it is functional and largely coherent. However, there are **critical security vulnerabilities** ([.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) not gitignored, Google Client ID committed in plaintext, dangerously loose URL validation, injection vectors), **architecture gaps** (no TypeScript despite the spec, duplicated pagination/filtering blocks, dynamic `import()` in hot paths, N+1 query risk), and **zero test coverage**. The sections below detail every finding with file references.

---

## 1. Architecture Review

### Folder Structure & Modularity

```
backend/
  Models/          ← PascalCase directory ✅
    application.model.js
    document.model.js
    payment.model.js
    roleAssignment.model.js
    studentList.model.js
    user.model.js
  Routes/          ← PascalCase with nested sub-dirs (mixed casing vs controllers/)
    Authentication/auth.routes.js
    Admin/admin.routes.js
    Admission_Cell/admissionCell.routes.js
    General_Office/generalOffice.routes.js
    Account_Office/accountOffice.routes.js
    Hod/hod.routes.js
    Students/student.routes.js
  controllers/     ← lowercase flat dir (inconsistent with Routes/)
    auth.controller.js | admin.controller.js | ...
  Services/        ← PascalCase
    Authentication/googleAuth.service.js
    Connections/db.connection.js
  middlewares/     ← lowercase (inconsistent with Services/)
  utils/           ← lowercase ✅
  index.js         ← entry point
```

**Issues:**
- Directory naming is inconsistent: `Models/` vs `controllers/`, `Routes/` vs `middlewares/`. JavaScript is case-insensitive on Windows but **case-sensitive on Linux/Docker** — this will cause `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` errors if deployed to any Linux server.
- Routes are nested one level deep per role (e.g., [Routes/Admin/admin.routes.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/Routes/Admin/admin.routes.js)) but [index.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/index.js) imports `from "./routes/auth.routes.js"` (lowercase `r`) — this works on Windows but will fail on Linux.
- There is no `src/` or `app/` root — all folders sit at the same level as [index.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/index.js) and `node_modules/`, which is unconventional and will make adding build tooling harder later.

### Controller / Service / Model Separation

- ✅ Controllers are clean — they call models directly and return responses. No business logic bleeds into routes.
- ❌ The "service layer" only has 2 files ([googleAuth.service.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/Services/Authentication/googleAuth.service.js), [db.connection.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/Services/Connections/db.connection.js)). All application logic (creating applications, matching students, verifying payments) lives in controllers, not services. For this scale it is acceptable but will not scale.
- ❌ [application.model.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/Models/application.model.js) references `documentSchema` on line 50 (`{ type: documentSchema, default: () => ({}) }`) before it is defined. `documentSchema` is declared in [document.model.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/Models/document.model.js) which is a separate file — this is a **broken reference** that will cause a runtime schema error.

### API Route Organization

- ✅ Routes are well-prefixed: `/api/auth`, `/api/student`, `/api/admission-cell`, etc.
- ✅ Each role group has its own router file with middleware applied at the router level.
- ⚠️ There is a semantic inconsistency: `generalOffice` controller handles both application review **and** role assignment (a concern that belongs in `admin`). This creates a privilege escalation risk (see Security section).
- ⚠️ No API versioning (e.g., `/api/v1/`). Any breaking change will affect all clients immediately.

### Middleware Design

| Middleware | What it does | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `verifyGoogleToken` | Validates Google ID token on every request | ✅ Re-validates with Google every request — correct, no stale JWT risk |
| [requireRole(...)](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/middlewares/auth.middleware.js#42-55) | Checks `req.user.role` against allowed list | ✅ Simple, correct |
| [errorHandler](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/middlewares/error.middleware.js#7-24) | Global error boundary | ✅ Good pattern, hides stack in prod |
| [notFoundHandler](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/middlewares/error.middleware.js#1-6) | 404 catcher | ✅ |

- ❌ No **rate limiter** middleware. The login endpoint (`POST /api/auth/google`) can be hammered with no throttling — brute-forceable indirectly via Google token replay attempts.
- ❌ No **body size limit** beyond Express's default. `uploadStudentList` accepts an unbounded `students` array in the JSON body — a large payload will stress the process.
- ❌ No **request ID / correlation ID** middleware. Debugging production issues is very difficult without it.
- ❌ No **Helmet.js** for HTTP security headers.

### Error Handling Strategy

- ✅ [asyncHandler](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/utils/asyncHandler.js#1-6) wraps all controller functions — uncaught promise rejections are forwarded to [errorHandler](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/middlewares/error.middleware.js#7-24).
- ✅ [ApiError](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/utils/ApiError.js#1-8) class with `statusCode` is used consistently in controllers.
- ⚠️ [sendError](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/utils/apiResponse.js#9-16) in [auth.middleware.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/middlewares/auth.middleware.js) sends responses directly (bypasses [errorHandler](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/middlewares/error.middleware.js#7-24)). This means auth errors are formatted differently from API errors thrown via [ApiError](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/utils/ApiError.js#1-8). Prefer `next(new ApiError(...))` everywhere for uniform error shape.
- ⚠️ [error.middleware.js](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/middlewares/error.middleware.js) uses `console.error(err)` — in production this can leak stack traces to log aggregators. Use a proper logger (Winston/Pino) with log levels.
- ❌ The global error handler checks `NODE_ENV` for `"development"` but the [.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) file has **no `NODE_ENV` set** — it defaults to `undefined`, which means `isDevelopment` is always `false` and error messages are hidden even locally.

### Scalability & Maintainability

- ❌ **No TypeScript** — the spec says TypeScript but the entire backend is plain JavaScript. There is no `tsconfig.json`, no type annotations, and no compile step.
- ❌ **No test files** anywhere in the backend. Zero coverage.
- ⚠️ Single-process Node.js with no clustering or process manager config. Fine for dev, risky for production.

