## 3. Security Review

### 🔴 Critical: [.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) Not Gitignored

```
# backend/.gitignore — only contains:
/node_modules
```

**The [.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) file is NOT gitignored.** It contains:
```
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=109146673866-lnbm2ulr5pndlidddi3d6hl94cs3rrs6.apps.googleusercontent.com
DBURL=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/MITS
```

Pushing this repository to GitHub will **expose the Google OAuth client ID and database URL** to anyone with repo access. Immediately add [.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) to [.gitignore](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.gitignore).

> **Action Required:** 
> 1. Add [.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) to [.gitignore](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.gitignore) now.
> 2. Check `git log` to see if [.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) was ever committed. If so, rotate the Google Client ID immediately.

### 🔴 Critical: No `JWT_SECRET` / Token Signing

The backend does not issue any JWT. Instead, it re-verifies the Google ID token with Google on **every request**. This means:
- Every authenticated API call makes an outbound HTTPS call to Google's token verification endpoint.
- If Google's servers are slow or unavailable, all authenticated routes become unavailable.
- There is no ability to issue org-specific access tokens (e.g., with custom claims like `role`).

**Recommended:** Issue a signed JWT on login (`/api/auth/google`) and verify it locally on subsequent requests.

### 🔴 Critical: Unvalidated URLs Stored Directly to DB

```js
// student.controller.js:61
const { docType, fileUrl, fileName, mimeType } = req.body;
if (!docType || !fileUrl) throw new ApiError(400, "docType and fileUrl are required");
// fileUrl is stored directly without any format or domain validation
```

```js
// student.controller.js:114
const { challanFileUrl, paymentMode, transactionId, amount } = req.body;
// challanFileUrl stored without validation
```

Any string (including `javascript:`, `data:`, or internal server addresses) can be stored as a document or payment URL. This enables **Stored XSS via malicious URL**, **SSRF if the backend ever fetches these URLs**, and data integrity issues.

**Fix:** Validate that URLs match an expected domain (e.g., your CDN/storage bucket) using `URL` parsing + allowlist.

### 🔴 Critical: Role Escalation via General Office

```js
// generalOffice.routes.js:14
router.use(verifyGoogleToken, requireRole("generalOffice", "administrator"));
// generalOffice.controller.js — assignRole endpoint
// POST /api/general-office/roles
// A generalOffice user can assign ANY role including "administrator"
```

The `assignRole` function allows `generalOffice` staff to assign anyone (including themselves) the `administrator` role. **This is a privilege escalation vulnerability.** Role assignment should be restricted to `administrator` only.

### 🔴 Critical: Google Auth Service Token Swallowed

```js
// googleAuth.service.js:35-37
} catch (error) {
    throw new ApiError(401, "Invalid or expired Google token");
}
```

The catch-all discards the original error. This masks legitimate errors such as missing `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` (will surface as "Invalid or expired Google token") making debugging impossible. Also, `verifyIdToken` from `google-auth-library` already throws descriptive errors — you should check `error instanceof ApiError` before re-wrapping.

### 🟡 Medium: No Input Validation Library

There is no input validation on any request body. The [validateRequest](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/utils/validateRequest.js#3-14) utility exists but is **never used**. Fields like:
- `students` array in `uploadStudentList` — no schema validation per entry
- `amount`, `minLimit`, `maxLimit` in payment endpoints — basic number checks only
- `email` in `assignRole` — no format validation, stored directly into DB query
- `branch`, `status` query params — passed directly to MongoDB filter without enum validation

This opens NoSQL injection vectors. For example:
```json
// POST /api/general-office/roles
{ "email": { "$gt": "" }, "role": "administrator" }
```
Mongoose will sanitize this for simple finds, but without strict string validation, crafted objects can bypass application logic.

**Fix:** Use `zod` or `joi` for schema validation on all request bodies.

### 🟡 Medium: Sensitive Data in Responses

- `User.find(filter).select("-googleSub")` — correctly strips `googleSub` ✅
- But `Application.find()` returns the full document including all staff remarks (`remarksGeneralOffice`, `remarksAdmissionCell`, `remarksAccountOffice`) to **any role** that can query applications — including admission cell staff seeing account office remarks they shouldn't need.

**Fix:** Add per-role projection when populating/returning application documents.

### 🟡 Medium: No CSRF Protection

The API is stateless (no cookies) so traditional CSRF doesn't apply, but there is no `SameSite` cookie policy or `Origin` header validation. This is acceptable for a pure token-in-header API but should be documented.

### 🟢 Minor: MongoDB `DBURL` Uses localhost

```
DBURL="mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/MITS"
```

No authentication credentials. If this URL is ever exposed (and the [.env](file:///c:/Users/Varun/Mits/backend/.env) is not gitignored!), anyone with access could connect to the database directly. Add at minimum a username/password to the connection string in a production environment.

---

## 4. Performance Analysis

### Inefficient Queries

**1. N+1 in `getProgressOverview` (10 separate count queries)**
As noted above — replace with a single aggregation.

**2. Re-fetching the application in `uploadDocument` (student.controller.js:88)**
```js
// The app is already fetched at line 64, then updated at line 88 via ANOTHER query
await Application.findByIdAndUpdate(app._id, { ... });
```
Since the app is already in memory, modify it and call `app.save()` once instead of doing two DB round trips.

**3. HOD controller fetches user + role assignment on every request (3 endpoints)**
```js
// hod.controller.js — getAdmittedStudents, getAdmittedStudentDetail, getBranchStats
const hodUser = await User.findById(req.user.id);
const assignment = await RoleAssignment.findOne({ email: hodUser.email });
```
2 extra DB queries per HOD request. The branch could be stored in `req.user` during `verifyGoogleToken`, or the `RoleAssignment` lookup could be cached (e.g., in Redis or in-memory with a short TTL).

**4. `dataMatching` can be unbounded (admin.controller.js:181)**
```js
const lists = await StudentList.find(filter).lean(); // no limit!
```
If there are thousands of student lists, this loads everything into memory at once. Add `.limit()` and pagination.

**5. `verifyDocument` double-fetches the document (accountOffice.controller.js:60+63)**
```js
const existingDocument = await Document.findById(req.params.documentId); // existence check
// ...
const doc = await Document.findByIdAndUpdate(req.params.documentId, ...); // actual update
```
A single `findByIdAndUpdate` with `returnDocument: "before"` or `"after"` eliminates the first query.

### Missing Indexes

The following query patterns are used repeatedly without confirmed indexes:
- `Application.findOne({ student: req.user.id })` — needs `{ student: 1 }` index
- `Document.find({ application: app._id })` — needs `{ application: 1 }` index
- `Payment.findOne({ application: app._id })` — needs `{ application: 1 }` index
- `RoleAssignment.findOne({ email: hodUser.email })` — needs `{ email: 1 }` (already `unique: true` which creates an index ✅)
- `User.find({ email: { $in: emails } })` — needs `{ email: 1 }` index (unique constraint creates it ✅)

The `student` field on `Application` is `unique: true`, which creates an index. But `document.application` and `payment.application` have no index defined.

### No Caching

All read-heavy endpoints (`getOverview`, `getProgressOverview`, `getBranchStats`, `dataMatching`) hit MongoDB on every request with no caching. For an admin dashboard that might be viewed many times per minute, these should use:
- In-memory cache (e.g., `node-cache`) with 30–60 second TTL
- Or Redis for multi-process deployments

### Body Parser Risk

No body size limit is configured:
```js
app.use(express.json()); // default limit is 100kb
```
The `uploadStudentList` endpoint accepts an array of student objects in the JSON body. A 100kb JSON body with tightly packed student records could contain thousands of entries. Either enforce a lower body limit or move toward a proper file upload + parsing approach.

